{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head d0409b60b48afaab20d9092825c04a679cd16650 on the LA-1 branch, all 6 artifacts, for request d6694b74 under my promise 79366845. The implementation is LA-1 (promise e8855a6c), step 1 of `notes/2026-11-18-local-artifacts-simulator.md`. The reporting artifact is the LA-1 note.\n\nThe head is one commit on 9255e1f8. It merges cleanly onto origin main 80b8e104 to tree 661a15e4.\n\n## What I read\n\n- **`artifactsLocalOrigin` (`artifacts-host.ts`).** It accepts a string of at most 64 characters only when all of these hold:\n  - the protocol is `http:` and the hostname is exactly `127.0.0.1`;\n  - the port is explicit, from 1 to 99999 with no leading zero;\n  - the URL has no user, password, path, query or fragment;\n  - `url.origin` equals the input text.\n\n  The last rule refuses a trailing slash, port 80 (which `origin` drops) and any spelling that the parser folds.\n- **`ArtifactsProvider`.** Without `localOrigin`, `#origin` is `https://\u003cservice\u003e`, and `#transport` adds no field, so behavior is unchanged. With it, the constructor refuses any value that `artifactsLocalOrigin` rejects. Every remote URL then uses the local origin, and the Git requests use `transport: \"local\"`.\n- **`names.ts:47` at the head.** The `local` transport admits plain HTTP to `127.0.0.1` alone. It still refuses credentials, a query, a fragment and a non-canonical href. `[::1]` stays refused, as ruling e43feff9 accepts.\n- **`SiteTokenReuse`.** It takes an optional `{ localOrigin }` in a new last parameter and throws \"Site local origin\" on a bad value. The binding's `info().remote` must then match the local origin rather than `https://\u003chost\u003e`.\n- **Callers.** The only production constructors are `artifacts-wiring.ts:112` and `:151`, `apps/room/archive.ts:43` and `worker.ts:153`. Each one lists its fields literally, and no source outside the two changed files names `localOrigin`. The new test \"no deployed wiring passes the local option\" pins that for `artifacts-wiring.ts`, `worker.ts` and `archive.ts`. The deployed config parser refuses a `localOrigin` field.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- **The reds.** `2026-11-18-la1-red/red.txt` (`53fe2c5d…`) and `green.txt` (`9ece0a95…`) match the note. On base source, the two option tests fail and the others pass, 2 of 22. On the head, 22 of 22 pass. The guard tests pass on both, as they should.\n- **The gate.** END 9e60a72d passed. `2026-11-18-la1-gate/gate.txt` names this head and tree f94ac72a with 0 changed files. It ends \"gate passed\" and \"exit=0\", and it hashes to `81bccaf4`.\n- **The seals.** The head moves `artifacts-host.ts` to `c0fb2c88` and `site/token-reuse.ts` to `f13f69f6`. I read both diffs in full.\n- **Scope.** The 6 artifacts filed at this head equal `git diff --no-renames --name-only 9255e1f8 d0409b60`.\n- **Cheap checks.** The commit-message check passes (1 checked, 0 flagged), `check-notes` gives 0 warnings, and `git diff --check` is clean. No added line names a local path.\n- **Wake rate.** The head adds no alarm, retry or schedule, and changes no Worker wiring.\n\n## Non-blocking\n\n- Both seal comments still describe their earlier reviews. Add \"LA-1: optional loopback origin for the simulator, code-only\" to each the next time either file changes.\n\nArchitecture: sound. The simulator plugs in through one optional constructor field and the existing `local` Git transport. No new code path reaches production.\n\nSecurity: no finding. Only code can pass the option, the deployed config cannot name it, and a test fails if a deployed wiring file mentions it. The accepted origin is exactly one loopback IPv4 address with an explicit port. With the option set, a binding that answers with an `https://\u003cservice\u003e` remote is refused, so a development room cannot drift onto the real service.\n\nSimplification: no required change.\n\nFact for the planner: no Worker byte changes behavior, because nothing deployed passes the option. A